skills/umbrella/scripts/helpers.md:23:2. **One redacted single-line warning per `EDGES_FAILED` event** of the form `**⚠ /umbrella: wire-dag edge BLOCKER->BLOCKED failed (HTTP STATUS): REASON**`. Used for both operational (HTTP 4xx/5xx, id-lookup) and policy-driven (`bound-exhausted`) failures — the `STATUS` placeholder carries the typed reason. The "HTTP" prose is a soft misnomer for the policy-driven case; preserved verbatim so the existing per-edge stderr count assertion (`grep -c '⚠ /umbrella: wire-dag edge'`) remains stable. The reason snippet is piped through `scripts/redact-secrets.sh` (canonical secret scrubber) when present; a degraded-layout fallback flattens to `tr | head -c 200` and emits a one-time process-local notice.
skills/umbrella/scripts/helpers.md:26:5. **One-time-per-run** warning for transient/operational repo-wide probe failure (issue #728): `**⚠ /umbrella: wire-dag probe failed (HTTP STATUS): REASON**`. Fires when the probe classified `PROBE_FAILED=1` — i.e., 5xx after retry, no-status after retry, 429, or other non-fingerprinted 4xx. Mirrors prefix #2's redact-secrets fail-closed discipline (substitutes `<REDACTION_FAILED>` placeholder when `redact-secrets.sh` exits non-zero; never prints the raw flattened body). One probe per run, so this warning fires at most once. When `PROBE_FAILED=1`, prefix #1 (the legacy "API not available" warning) does NOT fire — only the new prefix #5 emits — to avoid double-warning on the same condition. The shared `REDACT_FALLBACK_WARNED` one-time guard is intentionally per-run (layout-context, not call-site-context): if the probe path trips the fallback notice first, later per-edge failures inherit the same notice without re-emitting it.
skills/umbrella/scripts/helpers.md:28:**Residual redaction risk**: `scripts/redact-secrets.sh` covers token-shaped secrets (API keys, JWTs, PEM blocks, common provider key prefixes) but does NOT cover PII, internal hostnames, opaque bearer tokens, or DB connection strings (see the script's header for the explicit non-coverage list). Operators should treat these warnings like any other internal log line — best-effort secret scrubbing is not comprehensive PII protection.
skills/umbrella/scripts/helpers.md:50:Stderr: one redacted single-line warning per `TITLES_FAILED` event of the form `**⚠ /umbrella: prefix-titles edit #N failed (CODE): REASON**` where `CODE` is `exit <rc>` for `gh` non-zero exits (most operational failures: rate-limit, auth, 404, permission), or `input` for caller bugs in the children file (missing title column, OR a non-numeric / non-positive issue-number column — both bucket as `input` because the `gh issue edit` was not attempted). The reason snippet is captured from `gh`'s combined stderr (max 200 bytes), flattened (newlines / CRs → spaces), and piped through `scripts/redact-secrets.sh` when present; degraded-layout fallback uses inline `tr | head -c 200` with a one-time process-local notice. The redaction discipline mirrors `wire-dag`'s — failure of `redact-secrets.sh` substitutes a constant `<REDACTION_FAILED>` placeholder rather than printing the raw flattened body.
skills/umbrella/scripts/helpers.md:99:Why the refactor was invariant-preserving cleanup, not a fix for an active live bug: with numeric-only cache keys, `declare -A NAME 2>/dev/null || true` silently degrading to an indexed array on Bash 3.2 happens to produce equivalent membership/value-retrieval semantics for the existing call sites. The pre-#744 test suite passes on Bash 3.2.57. The refactor matters because (a) the cross-skill portability invariant is documented in `allocate-candidates.md` and the umbrella code was relying on the indexed-array-fallback accident; (b) defense-in-depth — a future contributor adding a non-numeric or composite key would silently produce wrong behavior under the pre-fix code; (c) the `2>/dev/null || true` mask was a code smell that obscured intent.
