scripts/merge-pr.md:54:As part of OID mismatch handling for step 1, the script checks whether local HEAD is ahead of the PR head OID exclusively by `chore(larch-logs): flush` commits (up to 5 ahead commits), whether the aggregate diff for `PR_HEAD_OID..HEAD` stays under `larch-logs/`, and whether the PR head OID is still an ancestor of local HEAD. This condition arises when `larch-log-flush.sh` tail calls fire after `gh pr create`, advancing local HEAD beyond the OID GitHub recorded for the PR.
docs/review-agents.md:102:**Note A — `/implement` Step 5 (public mirror)**: Step 5 invokes `run-step5-review.sh`, which forwards `--round-cap` derived from `POST_PLAN_WORKFLOW_PATH` (base cap **5** plus prior degraded rounds; orchestrator text refers to the inflated value as `effective_round_cap`) and does **not** forward `--panel` on the public argv — the panel is applied only inside `review-and-fix.sh` → `review-core.sh`. Up to the capped multi-round loop, the **review panel** with **6 Cursor specialists** (plus optional dynamic archetypes). Voting panel: **3-judge panel on round 1** (Claude opus + Codex + Cursor; Claude replacement when an external is unhealthy); **2-judge panel on rounds 2+** (Claude + Cursor; Codex voter omitted). Full voting-panel collapse thresholds for other skills remain authoritative in `skills/shared/voting-protocol.md`.
scripts/test-implement-rebase-macro.md:3:`scripts/test-implement-rebase-macro.sh` is the structural regression guard for the `/implement` Rebase Checkpoint Macro refactor (closes #232). Seven assertions (A, B, C, E, F, G, H): (A) exactly one `## Rebase Checkpoint Macro` header in `skills/implement/SKILL.md`; (B) the four canonical Call-site registry table rows are present — `(1.r, design plan)`, `(4.r, commit (impl))`, `(7.r, commit (review))`, `(7a.r, diagrams)`; (C) exactly four `Apply the Rebase Checkpoint Macro with <step-prefix>=X and <short-name>=Y.` invocation lines matching the canonical pairs; (E) Step 7.r section retains `FILES_CHANGED=true` prose ABOVE the macro invocation line within the Step 7 slice; (F) the macro header line number is strictly between the `### Verbosity Control` header line and the `<!-- step:0 — Session Setup -->` anchor line (placement constraint); (G) within the macro section line range (macro header through the Step 0 anchor), the `rebase-push.sh --no-push --skip-if-pushed --keep-on-conflict` invocation, `caller_kind=early_rebase` conflict-resolution dispatch, and non-conflict bail string appear; (H) exactly one `rebase-push.sh --no-push --skip-if-pushed --keep-on-conflict` occurrence in the entire file — residual inline rebase blocks would push the count to 2+ and fail; also the `rebase-push.sh --no-push` (without `--skip-if-pushed`) call sites summed across `skills/implement/SKILL.md` + `skills/implement/references/rebase-rebump-subprocedure.md` must equal **1** after issue #2485 removed Step 1.m's pre-design rebase from SKILL.md (only the sub-procedure prose site remains on that combined surface; Step 8b's rebase moved into `implement-finalize.sh` postbump Phase 3 per issue #1493). Step 7.r's `FILES_CHANGED=true` guard stays at the call site (macro owns HOW, call site owns WHETHER). Wired into `make lint` via the `test-implement-rebase-macro` target. The test script is added to `agent-lint.toml`'s exclude list because agent-lint's dead-script rule does not follow Makefile-only references. Edits to the `## Rebase Checkpoint Macro` section, the Call-site registry table, the four `Apply ...` invocation lines, or Step 7.r's `FILES_CHANGED=true` guard must stay in sync with the harness's literal assertions.
scripts/redact-tmpdir-paths.md:28:When changing the accepted tempdir roots or session prefix list, update `scripts/session-setup.sh`, `scripts/cleanup-tmpdir.sh`, `scripts/implement-finalize.sh`, `scripts/token-tally.sh`, this contract, and `scripts/test-redact-tmpdir-paths.sh`.
docs/workflow-lifecycle.md:12:    IMPLEMENT -->|runs helper for| STEP5["run-step5-review.sh"]
docs/workflow-lifecycle.md:34:- **`/implement`** — top-level orchestrator. Runs the full design → code → review → PR workflow by default; Step 5 invokes `run-step5-review.sh`, which derives `effective_round_cap` from base cap **5** plus degraded-round inflation, does **not** forward `--panel` on the public argv, and applies the panel only inside `review-and-fix.sh` → `review-core.sh` (see [Review Agents](review-agents.md) Note A for the **3-judge panel on round 1**, **review panel**, and **6 Cursor specialists** contract). With the `--merge` flag, also runs the CI+rebase+merge loop and local cleanup after PR creation. Step 0.5 resolves tracking-issue state (sentinel reuse, positional issue adoption, or `Closes #<N>` recovery from the current branch's PR body); committed larch-log batches are the single source of truth for full report content (voting tallies, rejected findings, version-bump reasoning, diagrams, OOS observation links, execution issues, run statistics), with the PR body as a slim projection (Summary + diagrams + Test plan + `Closes #<N>` — diagrams appear in both places by design). Step 9a.1 additionally invokes `/issue` in batch mode to file accepted OOS findings as GitHub issues.
scripts/round-trip-detect.md:3:`scripts/round-trip-detect.sh` is the pure detector for the managed <code>[ROUND-TRIP] </code> title marker. Primary callers are `/implement` Step 0.5, Step 12a/12b, `scripts/implement-finalize.sh` Step 18, `/fix-issue` Step 3/6, and `skills/fix-issue/scripts/finalize-umbrella.sh`.
docs/skills.md:95:Full implementation workflow spanning design through PR merge. Preflight consumes the **positional** GitHub `<issue-N>` after `/design` has written `larch:plan` into that issue's body. Step 5 invokes `run-step5-review.sh`, which derives `effective_round_cap` from base cap **5** plus degraded-round inflation, does **not** forward `--panel` on the public argv, and applies the panel only inside `review-and-fix.sh` → `review-core.sh`: a **3-judge panel on round 1** (Claude opus + Codex + Cursor; Claude replacement when an external is unhealthy) and a **2-judge panel on rounds 2+** (Claude + Cursor; Codex voter omitted), with the **review panel** and **6 Cursor specialists** (plus optional dynamic archetypes). `--merge` enables CI+merge; `--forked` is mutually exclusive with `--merge`.
scripts/restore-finalize-state.md:3:`scripts/restore-finalize-state.sh` rebuilds `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/finalize-state.sh` from `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/ship-pr-state.sh` immediately before `/implement` Step 18 invokes `scripts/implement-finalize.sh teardown`.
scripts/restore-finalize-state.md:7:`ship-pr.sh` writes `finalize-state.sh` during postmerge, but Step 18 can still run after a prompt-side interruption or stale partial file. This helper treats `ship-pr-state.sh` as the authoritative checkpoint and rewrites the teardown input atomically so cleanup, tracking-issue rename, manifest finalization, and tmpdir removal consume the latest state.
scripts/restore-finalize-state.md:19:The helper never sources `ship-pr-state.sh`. It reads values with the same `awk` key lookup pattern as `ship-pr.sh`, preserving literal shell metacharacters after the first `=`.
scripts/restore-finalize-state.md:21:`scripts/lib-finalize-state-keys.sh` owns the canonical finalize-state key order shared by `ship-pr.sh` and this helper. The shared list contains:
scripts/restore-finalize-state.md:46:Only `DESIGN_ONLY_DONE` has a non-empty default, `false`, for compatibility with older or partial `ship-pr-state.sh` files. Other missing keys are written as empty values. `BAIL_REASON` is not part of `finalize-state.sh`; it is copied to `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/final-bail-reason.txt`, matching `ship-pr.sh`.
scripts/restore-finalize-state.md:66:When changing finalize-state keys, defaults, or `BAIL_REASON` handling, update `scripts/lib-finalize-state-keys.sh`, `scripts/ship-pr.sh`, `scripts/restore-finalize-state.sh`, `scripts/test-restore-finalize-state.sh`, `scripts/implement-finalize.md`, and `skills/implement/SKILL.md` together.
